{"_provenance": "Columbus, Ohio crime data, 49 planning neighbourhoods. Source: the `columbus` data of the R package spdep (github.com/r-spatial/spdep, data/columbus.rda), from Anselin (1988), Spatial Econometrics: Methods and Models, with `col.gal.nb`, the queen contiguity neighbours list distributed with them (115 undirected edges, degrees 2-10). Model: the textbook spatial-lag regression CRIME ~ INC + HOVAL used by spdep's own lagsarlm documentation. The response CRIME and both regressors are z-scored (a sampler-conditioning choice that rescales beta and sigma; it does not change the model). Two fixed weight matrices, both data: W = D^-1 B row-standardised, the lag operator; Ws = D^-1/2 B D^-1/2 symmetric normalised adjacency, similar to W and so of identical spectrum, used for the log-determinant so it can be a Cholesky of a symmetric positive definite matrix (Ord 1975; spdep's `similar.listw`); identity checked to 6.04e-14. eig(Ws) = [-0.651955, 1.000000], stationary rho in (-1.5338, 1). This n=49 instance is the disclosed fallback for the DotScale row after the n=506 Boston instance proved impractical to measure (its SICM-disabled arm exceeded 40 min per trial). Nothing is simulated. Built by benchmark/probprog/ablation/sicm_roster/build_columbus_sar.py.", "N": 49, "K": 3, "feature_names": ["(Intercept)", "INC", "HOVAL"], "y": [-1.1716356150379357, -0.9859058126873261, -0.27185467385182566, -0.1655184210538067, 0.9421640880866552, -0.5472164993580134, -2.1104800460768867, 0.19909053529786636, -0.2785491672729281, -0.06811331231476737, 1.6392417417399117, 1.30291210477485, 0.6996963646803215, 1.3246785680275819, 0.81257705482938, 1.1901763379932566, 0.10506642833705065, 0.5334183578650806, 1.1710497142636371, -2.107730849796075, 0.2986176240602765, -0.08597433235326193, -0.910621142526052, 0.19136207403463731, 1.58028975393774, 0.3527023001813974, 1.0667329685912434, 1.3158414430032448, 1.5471548978936849, 2.0387831473548346, -1.0538108642078066, -0.96514324567